Day 13 - S3 Versioning

1. S3 Versioning

S3 Versioning is a feature that keeps different versions of the same object in a bucket.

It helps protect data from accidental changes or deletions because older versions can be preserved.

2. How Versioning Works

When Versioning is enabled, uploading the same object again creates a new version instead of simply replacing the old version.

For example:

index.html
├── Version 1
├── Version 2
└── Version 3

Each version has its own unique Version ID.

Versioning is enabled at the bucket level.

3. Multiple Versions of an Object

The same S3 object can have multiple versions.

For example, if "index.html" is uploaded three times with different content, S3 can keep all three versions:

Version 1 → My First S3 Static Website
Version 2 → My S3 Versioning Practice
Version 3 → Updated Website

This allows older versions to remain available for recovery.

4. Accidental Deletion and Recovery

When Versioning is enabled, deleting an object normally creates a Delete Marker instead of immediately removing the previous version.

The older versions can still remain in the bucket.

The Delete Marker can be removed to make the previous object version accessible again.

Delete Object
      ↓
Delete Marker
      ↓
Old Version Still Available
      ↓
Remove Delete Marker
      ↓
Object Recovered

5. Versioning Practical

For the practical:

1. Enabled Versioning on the S3 bucket.
2. Used the existing "index.html" file.
3. Changed the content of "index.html".
4. Uploaded the same "index.html" again.
5. Selected Replace when S3 detected that the file already existed.
6. Used Show versions to verify that multiple versions of "index.html" were stored.

This demonstrated how S3 keeps different versions of the same object.


